\title{The Circle as a Quotient of an Interval}
\taxon{Example}
\p{
    Let
    ##{
        S^1=\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid |z|=1\},
        \qquad q:[0,1]\longrightarrow S^1,
        \qquad q(t)=e^{2\pi i t}.
    }
    The map #{q} identifies precisely the endpoints #{0} and #{1}. Let
    #{p:[0,1]\to[0,1]/(0\sim1)} be the canonical quotient map. There is a
    unique bijection
    ##{
        \bar q:[0,1]/(0\sim1)\longrightarrow S^1
    }
    such that #{\bar q\circ p=q}.
}
\p{
    Since #{q} is continuous, the [universal property of the quotient](thm-001F)
    makes #{\bar q} continuous. The interval #{[0,1]} is compact by
    [compactness of closed intervals](thm-001P), and its quotient is compact by
    [preservation under continuous images](thm-001J). The circle is Hausdorff
    as a subspace of the Euclidean plane. Therefore the
    [compact-to-Hausdorff criterion](thm-001L) shows that #{\bar q} is a
    homeomorphism:
    ##{
        [0,1]/(0\sim1)\cong S^1.
    }
}
